Commentary of al-Nasafi (Madarik al-Tanzil) on surah 47, verse 31:

﴿And We will surely test you﴾, with fighting, (as) an announcement (to you), not as a seeking of information; or We deal with you in the manner of one who tests, so that it is more emphatic in manifesting justice;

﴿until We know those of you who strive (in jihad) and the steadfast﴾, in jihad, i.e., We will know what exists as We have known what will be;

﴿and We will test your reports﴾, your secrets. "And We will surely test you until We know... and test," (is read by) "Abu Bakr". And it is related from al-Fudayl that whenever he recited it he would weep and say: "O Allah, do not test us, for if You test us You will expose us, tear away our veils, and punish us."

Commentary of al-Nasafi (Madarik al-Tanzil) on surah 47, verse 35:

﴿So do not weaken﴾, i.e., do not become feeble, and do not be humbled before the enemy;

﴿and call to peace﴾, with kasr (the vowel kasra on the initial letter, reading tad'ū as tadʿū with a different vowel pattern), read by Hamzah and Abu Bakr, meaning: al-musalamah (mutual peace), i.e., do not call the unbelievers to reconciliation (ṣulḥ);

﴿while you are the superior ones﴾, i.e., the victorious ones. The word "tadʿū" is jazm (in the jussive mood) because it falls under the ruling of the prohibition (nahy);

﴿and Allah is with you﴾, with help, i.e., your helper;

﴿and He will not deprive you of your deeds﴾, i.e., He will not diminish for you the reward of your deeds.
